ir putin  well i i don't favor anything that makes  us more reliant on imports and i  certainly don't favor anything that you  know would compromise people's jobs and  livelihoods but we face a climate  emergency you know i'm here in irvine  the town i grew up in as it happens not  far from glasgow that just  a matter of weeks ago hosted cop26 we  all have an obligation a moral  obligation to face up to the climate  emergency and for scotland the uk  countries across the world that means  transitioning away from fossil fuels but  we need to do that in a way that  protects jobs that protects livelihoods  that doesn't increase our reliance on  imports so that's a careful  journey that we have to undertake but  scotland as today's announcement is  really uh illustrating foreign is in the  happy fortunate position of not just  having had oil and gas resources for  several decades now but also having this  abundance of renewable energy resources  well our business correspondent paul  kelso joins me now so paul in the last  month around a quarter of our energy  supplies come from wind so how much  difference will these contracts make  in the long term they'll make a very big  difference and no wonder the first  minister was so excited about this so  she's right this is a huge  commitment to renewable particularly  wind specifically wind power in scotland  who's going to net her government  ultimately 700 million pounds this  auction by the crown estate scotland who  own the seabed so they've leased uh this  seabed for this use the big uh  technological shift here which the first  minister didn't mention is the bulk of  these projects are floating wind farms  for the first time the majority almost  all of the wind power that's already  generating if you say a quarter around a  quarter of you the uk's energy  electricity supplies at the moment is  fixed and there's a limit ultimately to  the engineering to how far off offshore  you can go once once you are as long as  you are still tethered to the ocean bed  about 165 feet uh deep about 30 40  meters is the limit at the moment now  floating offshore wind opens up much  greater distances offshore where the  wind speeds are much much higher and the  north sea is a savage place extremely  high wind the more wind the more power  you can generate so that's where the  real dividend from this new technology  will be i spoke to keith anderson the  chief executive of scottish power which  has won three of these sites it will do  two of them in a joint partnership which  shell of course who have experience in  the north sea's last great energy boom  which was oil and he said there is uh he  described this almost as a no-lose  project  so this is great for several reasons  number one  it moves us away from volatile wholesale  markets and gas prices  number two  it delivers us closer and closer to net  zero by 2050 and number three it  produces a fixed price for power at some  of the lowest prices possible in the  entire market so renewable energy is  cheaper and it's the cheapest form of  generation in the united kingdom  now this will take time to deliver work  will start on these floating offshore  wind farms in the late uh at the end of  this decade and could take up to another  decade to be completed but it's crucial  if the uk is to transition fully to  renewables and away from the source of  this current anxiety about massively  spiking energy prices which is the  reliance on gas to bridge this gap  between the decline of coal because of  its carbon emissions and a renewable  future but the north sea once the source  of all that oil could soon be the source  of wind power which could power not just  the uk  but much of europe  paul thanks very much indeed  so let's take a look now some of the  day's other climate news and huge brands  including coca-cola and nestle are  calling for an end to plastic pollution  the businesses have signed an open  letter recommending a legally binding  plast