sses which can which have to  address the climate crisis in a number  of different ways  but it can't do everything and the real  and i think um the cop process through  the paris agreement which is flawed in a  number of different ways nonetheless  sets some of those standards where the  real deficit is coming as a national  action and sub-national action corporate  action that's where we're really not  seeing the follow-through so if you look  at um something called the climate  action tracker which has a rating system  of national actions  practically no northern uh states which  is what we're principally talking about  have a standard of action which is  regarded as almost  sufficient or  uh paris compatible none do in fact the  united kingdom is the only country which  is almost sufficient most of the rest of  the global north is highly insufficient  so that's really where we need to see  the action  at that level and corporates need to  follow  you know what all that was said about  system change i entirely agree with  there's no question that there is in  order to meet any form of meaningful  climate action there is a need of system  change which means a lot more than  driving a electric car and  eating less meat it's fundamental  changes to the way we produce and  consume in exchange okay and there's  several hours worth of debate to discuss  how that will work which we don't have  time for right now of course now fresh  singh zayatong always good to hear your  views thanks both very much indeed for  joining us tonight  well tomorrow i'll be joined by the  director of policy and translation at  the grantham institute alyssa gilbert  and professor of global change science  at university college london and the  university of leeds simon lewis but for  now thanks for watching and i'll see you  next time  [Music]  you

