raffic  offenses sexual violence accessing  information on electronic devices so  it's a massive bill hundreds and  hundreds of pages long and what one  thing that that means is that it's  very difficult to scrutinize  um and the constitution committee if the  house of lords made exactly this point  um has made the point before but the  government ignored that  advice so procedurally  it's a bit of a dog's dinner um  substantively there are some really  challenging  uh uh issues uh or approaches that the  bill has taken i think the first of  these is that it  has this um you know we're in the realm  of public order here and peaceful  protest um and it has this idea of a  noise trigger so noise triggers can if  there's an uh that can be a condition  uh which uh police officers  uh can impose on a peaceful protest if  the if there's a noise trigger we know  that protests are often noisy people  bang drums or their fpa systems and so  on um and the idea that that can be the  basis for  restricting a protest i think is very  troubling and it's very likely that that  will contravene  either the european convention of human  rights  or the common law and most likely both  i just want to get some reaction from  zaya as well  sorry to interrupt there but um as i  we've got a real sense there haven't we  of some of navraj's concerns about it  but those who are behind this bill might  say well yeah we want people to protest  but disrupting the lives of people going  about their their everyday  lives shouldn't necessarily be allowed  and their this bill would tougher  sentences for blocking motorways or  other forms of transport for example  there are going to be people that will  support that aren't there  well there might be but i think we have  to look with a historical lens and we  have to realize that what we see as a  nuisance today is often seen through the  lens of history is something quite  monumental for progressive change so if  i think of the suffragettes who were  you know tying themselves and chaining  themselves to buildings and blocking  traffic i say thank you because of you i  as a woman have the right to vote today  if i think of the hay market protesters  who created a huge nuisance thank you  because of you i only work eight hours a  day instead of twelve if i look at for  example the clay quit sound protesters  who chained themselves to trees and  created a huge nuisance for the logging  industry thank you today we have old  growth forests but even if you look from  a geographic lens when we look overseas  and we see for example regimes we look  at say you know russia or china or the  middle east and we say they're quashing  descent and they're stopping their  protesters and we say that's  totalitarian well if it's totalitarian  overseas it's totalitarian as home at  home as well  navraj very quickly though i mean  do they actually make a difference  whether protests are peaceful or  disruptive i mean they may make people  talk about them but certainly if they're  stopping people going around their their  daily lives it can  it can put people off the argument can't  it very briefly if you can  for the avoidance of doubt we're only  talking about the realm of peaceful  protest there's nothing about disorder  here um  this is how issues get ventilated as you  just heard from the other guests issues  get ventilated in ways which are  sometimes inconvenient and inconvenience  as a central part of peaceful protest  okay let's move on to our next topic  shall we um to try and squeeze another  conversation in because the government's  admitted that it's not doing enough to  adapt to the impacts of climate change  that's in response to a report from the  climate change committee now the  government was also recently accused of  a lack of effective policy when it comes  to reaching net zero targets so can we  rely on governments to do the right  thing not just here but also  internationally is the global diplomatic  system equipped to tackle the climate  crisis so xia what do you think can  governments be relied on and if not  what's the alternative  well i think that uh the incremental  system change approach is certainly not  working if we look at almost 30 years of  cops we're seeing a situation that is a  much greater crisis today than when they  started the same thing even if you're  looking at the united nations  declaration on the rights for indigenous  people there was legislation passed in  bc where i'm from and still today we're  not seeing accountability we're not  seeing meaningful change we're seeing  land offenders and water defenders being  arrested by the rcmp the police we're  seeing pipelines being built on unseated  territory we're seeing forests being  raised so we need to start thinking  about a different type of system change  and you know we always think oh system  change is that even possible and we  forget colonialism fairly recent was a  form of system change you had europeans  sweeping across the rest of the world  exporting a model an idea that nature  was inert and an object that you could  go in there and you could destroy  exploit extract and bring it to market  and that is the system that has brought  us the crisis the climate crisis and the  extinction crisis that we face today so  we need to learn from a more reciprocal  model that we've seen from indigenous  people so system change really starts  with changing how we think about the  system okay enough do accept those  arguments let's let's deal with cop in  particular  zaya saying that it's not effective do  you think that's right because some  might say that the last cop did see the  big polluters coming on board with at  least some new commitments and a  commitment as well to come back later  this year to be held to account do you  agree with zaya or do you agree with  those people who stand up for that form  of diplomacy  cop has a role and  its role is to set a set of broad  standards  and processes which can which have to  address the climate crisis in a number  of different ways  but it can't do everything and the real  and i think um the cop process through  the paris agreement which is flawed in a  number of different ways nonetheless  sets some of those standards where the  real deficit is coming as a national  action and sub-national action corporate  action that's where we're really not  seeing the follow-through so if you look  at um something called the climate  action tracker which has a rating system  of national actions  practically no northern uh states which  is what we're principally talking about  have a standard of action which is  regarded as almost  sufficient or  uh paris compatible none do in fact the  united kingdom is the only country which  is almost sufficient most of the rest of  the global north is highly insufficient  so that's really where we need to see  the action  at that level and corporates need to  follow  you know what all that was said about  system change i entirely agree with  there's no question that there is in  order to meet any form of meaningful  climate action there is a need of system  change which means a lot more than  driving a electric car and  eating less meat it's fundamental  changes to the way we produce and  consume in exchange okay and there's  several hours worth of debate to discuss  how that will work which we don't have  time for right now of course now fresh  singh zayatong always good to hear your  views thanks both very much indeed for  joining us tonight  well tomorrow i'll be joined by the  director of policy and translation at  the grantham institute alyssa gilbert  and professor of global change science  at university college london and the  university of leeds simon lewis but for  now thanks for watching and i'll see you  next time  [Music]  you
 

